- Compare AI-generated responses head-to-head based on financial soundness, regulatory compliance, and analytical rigor.
    
- Review financial scenarios involving cash flow oversight, budgeting, pricing decisions, and financial reporting and provide expert feedback.
    

**Required Qualifications**

- **4+ years** of experience as a Financial Manager, Finance Director, Branch Manager, Financial Planning Manager, Financial Reporting Manager, or another closely related financial management role.
    
- Experience overseeing accounting, banking, credit, insurance, securities, or financial planning activities at the branch, office, or department level.
    
- Ability to commit **20+ hours per week**.
    
- Strong written English communication skills.
    

**Additional Information**

- Start date: Immediate
    
- Duration: Approximately 4–6 weeks
    
- Payment: task-based (payment upon first task approval). Experts who complete their first task within the required timeline will then be qualified to be paid hourly for the remainder of the project.


### Role Details & Eligibility
• Commitment: Hourly
• Eligible Locations: USA', '["Compliance", "Accounting"]', '["Contribute specialized domain expertise in Financial Managers", "Collaborate with leading frontier AI research teams to evaluate and improve model reasoning", "Ensure strict adherence to quality benchmarks and high-accuracy reference guidelines"]', 'open', '2026-11-04T21:48:50.407678Z', '2026-11-04T21:49:28.123610Z', 'https://work.mercor.com/jobs/list_AAABoCWSTbhgejc0oLZDmbSP?referralCode=2a6bc3e0-a5f7-40ad-95f3-1609d00a73a8');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO jobs (id, title, company, category, location, type, experience, compensation, description, skills, responsibilities, status, created_at, updated_at, external_url) VALUES ('b99d43c6-098a-59aa-bc3d-a7aa62066bea', 'Forensic / Toxicological Chemist', 'Mercor', 'Healthcare', 'Remote', 'Contract', 'Specialist / SME', '$75/hr', '## About the work

Mercor is assembling a panel of chemistry and chemical safety experts to red-team frontier AI models. The goal is to test whether a model can correctly judge the misuse potential of a technical request — answering legitimate questions fully while refusing genuinely dangerous ones.

You will:

- Write challenging single-turn prompts in your domain, labelled across three levels: benign, dual-use, and adversarial.
- Evaluate the responses that come back against a defined policy standard, and judge whether each was handled correctly.
- Write the reference answer — what a correct response looks like, and the technical reasoning for why.

This domain lives on the dual-use line more than most. The same casework knowledge that identifies a substance describes how it was produced; the same precursor list that supports an investigation is a shopping list read backwards. A model that refuses a routine identification or casework question is failing as badly as one that answers a question it shouldn''t. Drawing that line reliably is why we need practitioners rather than generalists.

## Background we''re looking for

We''re looking for people who have done casework — identifying substances and precursors, and defending the conclusion in a report or on the stand. What matters most is being able to tell a routine professional question from one that''s fishing for something else: the same precursor knowledge that identifies a clandestine route is the route. You will be writing prompts that sit exactly on that line, then judging whether the model held it.

Examples of relevant backgrounds (ideally with red-teaming experience):

- Controlled substance casework in a forensic laboratory — identification and quantitation
- Clandestine laboratory response, precursor identification and scene chemistry
- Forensic toxicology — postmortem or human performance casework
- Seized-drug and precursor trend monitoring or intelligence analysis
- Expert testimony and report writing on chemical casework

## What this role demands beyond technical depth

This is writing-intensive work. Every judgment you make needs a written rationale that a non-specialist can follow. Prior technical writing, published research, or expert witness experience is a strong signal; please include a sample or link. You will also be reading and writing about misuse scenarios in your field for sustained periods. We brief experts on this in advance, and you can pause or step away at any point without penalty.

## Before you apply

Your work here will not involve, and must not draw on, classified or export-controlled information, or anything covered by an NDA or prepublication review obligation. If you hold such obligations you may still be a good fit; tell us in your application and we will scope the work accordingly.


### Role Details & Eligibility
• Commitment: Task-Based
• Client Partner: Neon', '["Forensic Chemistry", "Toxicology", "Chemical Safety"]', '["Contribute specialized domain expertise in Forensic / Toxicological Chemist", "Collaborate with leading frontier AI research teams to evaluate and improve model reasoning", "Ensure strict adherence to quality benchmarks and high-accuracy reference guidelines"]', 'open', '2026-11-04T21:48:50.407678Z', '2026-11-04T21:49:28.123610Z', 'https://work.mercor.com/jobs/list_AAABoKHds2e4X1lxEltFyJa7?referralCode=2a6bc3e0-a5f7-40ad-95f3-1609d00a73a8');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO jobs (id, title, company, category, location, type, experience, compensation, description, skills, responsibilities, status, created_at, updated_at, external_url) VALUES ('ad1a08f0-c7df-5f72-a9a8-a95ba8edd13b', 'Forensic Scientist — Nuclear Materials', 'Mercor', 'Science', 'Remote', 'Contract', 'Specialist / SME', '$75/hr', '## About the work

Mercor is assembling a panel of nuclear materials and safeguards experts to red-team frontier AI models. The goal is to test whether a model can correctly judge the misuse potential of a technical request — answering legitimate questions fully while refusing genuinely dangerous ones.

You will:

- Write challenging single-turn prompts in your domain, labelled across three levels: benign, dual-use, and adversarial.
- Evaluate the responses that come back against a defined policy standard, and judge whether each was handled correctly.
- Write the reference answer — what a correct response looks like, and the technical reasoning for why.

This domain lives on the dual-use line more than most. The same signature that attributes a sample is the one someone would want to suppress; the same detection limit that catches material moving can be read as a threshold to stay under. A model that refuses a routine measurement-methodology question is failing as badly as one that answers a question it shouldn''t. Drawing that line reliably is why we need practitioners rather than generalists.

## Background we''re looking for

We''re looking for people who have characterised real nuclear material — detection, attribution, and the analytical work behind a defensible conclusion. What matters most is being able to tell a routine professional question from one that''s fishing for something else: the same signature that identifies a source tells you what to hide. You will be writing prompts that sit exactly on that line, then judging whether the model held it.

Examples of relevant backgrounds (ideally with red-teaming experience):

- Nuclear forensics casework or international exercise participation — sample characterisation and attribution
- Radiochemical and mass-spectrometric analysis — isotopics, age dating, impurity and process signatures
- Detection and interdiction technology — portal monitors, handheld identification, search operations
- Material signature libraries, reference materials and attribution methodology
- Pre- or post-detonation forensics research at a national laboratory or agency

## What this role demands beyond technical depth

This is writing-intensive work. Every judgment you make needs a written rationale that a non-specialist can follow. Prior technical writing, published research, or expert witness experience is a strong signal; please include a sample or link. You will also be reading and writing about misuse scenarios in your field for sustained periods. We brief experts on this in advance, and you can pause or step away at any point without penalty.

## Before you apply

Your work here will not involve, and must not draw on, classified or export-controlled information, or anything covered by an NDA or prepublication review obligation. If you hold such obligations you may still be a good fit; tell us in your application and we will scope the work accordingly.


### Role Details & Eligibility
• Commitment: Task-Based
• Client Partner: Neon', '["Forensic Chemistry", "Nuclear Engineering"]', '["Contribute specialized domain expertise in Forensic Scientist \u2014 Nuclear Materials", "Collaborate with leading frontier AI research teams to evaluate and improve model reasoning", "Ensure strict adherence to quality benchmarks and high-accuracy reference guidelines"]', 'open', '2026-11-04T21:48:50.407678Z', '2026-11-04T21:49:28.123610Z', 'https://work.mercor.com/jobs/list_AAABoKHbGAiriiwiUfZMAYrU?referralCode=2a6bc3e0-a5f7-40ad-95f3-1609d00a73a8');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO jobs (id, title, company, category, location, type, experience, compensation, description, skills, responsibilities, status, created_at, updated_at, external_url) VALUES ('1293a9ad-4967-588f-a803-1601505665b3', 'French Linguistic Experts', 'Mercor', 'Languages', 'Remote', 'Contract', 'Specialist / SME', '$50/hr', 'Mercor is seeking experienced French Linguistic Experts to join a cutting-edge AI research project in partnership with a leading research lab. This role centers on evaluating how effectively a large language model (LLM) performs in French-language tasks and on curating high-quality French linguistic data to train and improve the model''s understanding and generation capabilities.

**Key Responsibilities:**

- Evaluate the quality and accuracy of LLM-generated French text across various use cases (e.g., translation, summarization, dialogue, grammar correction).
    
- Edit, refine, or rewrite French content to meet linguistic and cultural standards.
    
- Annotate French text for grammatical, syntactic, and semantic features.
    
- Identify model errors and language gaps, and collaborate with AI researchers to improve LLM performance.
    
- Provide detailed linguistic feedback and analysis to guide ongoing model training efforts.
    

**You''re a strong fit if you have:**

- Native or near-native fluency in French, with excellent command over grammar, syntax, and stylistics.
    
- Professional experience in translation, editing, linguistic annotation, or NLP data work in French.
    
- Strong understanding of linguistic nuances, regional variations, and context-appropriate usage.
    
- Interest in AI and LLM-driven language technologies (preferred but not required).
    
- Excellent attention to detail and ability to spot subtle errors or unnatural phrasing.
    
- Clear communication skills and experience working in distributed, remote teams.
    

**Why Join**

- Contribute to cutting-edge AI research in partnership with a leading research lab.
    
- Work on high-impact projects that directly support the development of advanced AI systems.
    
- Gain hands-on exposure to state-of-the-art AI research and development.
    
