- **5+ years of professional software, firmware, FPGA, or embedded systems engineering experience**, including at least **3 years of recent, actively hands-on experience** developing, integrating, testing, bringing up, or troubleshooting software/firmware on real hardware—not solely program or people management.
    
- Demonstrated experience as the **responsible engineer for production hardware-connected software or firmware**, owning meaningful portions of the lifecycle from requirements and architecture through implementation, integration, hardware bring-up, verification, validation, debugging, and deployment.
    
- Deep technical expertise in at least one core domain: **embedded firmware and real-time systems; FPGA/RTL design and verification; flight software and safety/mission-critical systems; or hardware test automation and hardware-in-the-loop infrastructure**.
    
- Strong practical command of relevant technologies such as **C/C++, Python, RTOS/bare-metal development, device drivers, bootloaders, embedded Linux, SPI/I²C/UART/CAN/Ethernet, Verilog/SystemVerilog/VHDL, FPGA toolchains, hardware interfaces, telemetry, and automated test systems**, as applicable to the candidate''s specialization.
    
- Extensive hands-on experience **debugging software-hardware interactions and physical systems** using tools such as JTAG/SWD debuggers, logic analyzers, oscilloscopes, hardware simulators/emulators, HIL/SIL environments, automated test equipment, CI infrastructure, and structured root-cause analysis.
    
- Proven experience developing systems in **real-world product or hardware manufacturing environments**, ideally across aerospace/defense, space, automotive/EV, semiconductors, consumer electronics, robotics/eVTOL, industrial/energy, or medical devices—e.g., **Lockheed Martin, RTX, Boeing, Blue Origin, Rocket Lab, SpaceX, Tesla, Rivian, NVIDIA, Qualcomm, Intel, AMD, Apple, Google, Meta, Amazon, Skydio, Shield AI, Joby, Boston Dynamics, Siemens, ABB, Medtronic, or Intuitive Surgical**—with the ability to clearly explain architectural decisions, implementation trade-offs, test results, and failure mechanisms.
    

